//@version=6
// =============================================================================================
// NQ / MNQ — BOS/CHoCH.  v7
//
// ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
// │ BEFORE YOU READ THE RESULT — TradingView "Script execution" (the dropdown under the        │
// │ backtest report) must have ONLY  "On bar close"  ticked.                                   │
// │                                                                                            │
// │ Measured on a live MNQ 30m chart, same script, same period:                                │
// │      On bar close only ....  183 trades   +$9,380   PF 1.58   maxDD  2.59%                 │
// │      All four ticked .......  248 trades  −$16,247   PF 0.56   maxDD 16.61%                │
// │                                                                                            │
// │ This is not fragility. The rule is `close > pivot` — a break CONFIRMED at the bar's close. │
// │ "On order fill" / "history bar tick" / "realtime bar tick" re-run the script DURING the    │
// │ bar, so `close` becomes the running price and every intrabar poke through a pivot that     │
// │ reverses before the close fires a false BOS. Ticking those boxes does not make the test    │
// │ more realistic; it silently replaces this strategy with a worse one.                       │
// └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//
// TIMEFRAME MATTERS MORE THAN ANY SETTING. Use 30m or 60m. On 5m this rule LOSES: my engine
// gives PF 0.96-0.99 there and coin-flip entries with the same stop beat it 65% of the time. On
// 1m and 2m it is significantly negative (t = −2.45, −2.68). A losing 5-minute backtest is not a
// bug, it is the documented result.
//
// FIX HISTORY — every one of these was a real defect, found by diffing this script against the
// Python engine the research was done on:
//   v7  commission defaults to the MICRO fee. The sizing note below says to trade MNQ (a 2×ATR
//       stop on NQ risks ~$2,000); the fee was set at $2.00/order, right for the E-mini and 10×
//       too heavy on a Micro, where it ate ~5% of gross profit. Following both instructions as
//       written was self-defeating. TradingView will not let this be derived or made an input
//       (commission_value demands a const), so the label now checks it against the chart instead.
//   v6  THE ENTRY GATE (see grpT). A session-string window admitted the session's FIRST bar,
//       which the engine excludes. Worth 35 extra trades and −$8,318 on 30m. With it corrected
//       this script reproduces the engine exactly.
//   v6  volatility sizing defaulted ON while the reference figures are fixed-1-contract, so the
//       P&L could not match regardless of anything else. Now off, and under the spec lock.
//   v4  the run counter tested `bias` AFTER reassigning it, so it never reset on a structure
//       flip — silently turning "enter on the second BOS" into "enter on every BOS".
//   v2  the stop was measured from the SIGNAL bar's close but filled at the NEXT bar's open, so
//       every stop sat at the wrong distance. Now anchored to strategy.position_avg_price.
//
// DIAGNOSING A MISMATCH. Each statistic below identifies ONE input and is flat in the others.
// Net P&L is the worst diagnostic available — it moves with all of them at once.
//   win rate   -> stop multiple   2.0×ATR = 39-40%,  1.0 = 27-29%,  0.5 = 16-17%
//   trade count-> nBos            ~150-190 at 2,     ~290-400 at 1  (same window)
//   comm % of gross profit -> contract size   NQ 0.3-0.5%,  MNQ at $2.00/order 2.9-5.2%
// TradingView caches inputs PER CHART and a stale value survives a script update, which is what
// "LOCK the tested spec" exists for. Read the diagnostic label before believing any number.
// =============================================================================================

strategy("NQ BOS/CHoCH book", overlay = true, initial_capital = 100000,
     default_qty_type = strategy.fixed, default_qty_value = 1,
     commission_type = strategy.commission.cash_per_order,
     // TradingView requires a CONST here. It cannot be derived from syminfo.pointvalue and it
     // cannot even be an input -- both are rejected as "simple float" where "const float" is
     // expected. So it is set by hand, and the diagnostic label checks it against the chart:
     //     MNQ / MES  (Micro,  $2 / $5  per point) ....  0.50
     //     NQ  / ES   (E-mini, $20 / $50 per point) ...  2.00
     // Defaulted to the Micro because the sizing note at the foot of this file tells you to trade
     // it -- a 2xATR stop on full-size NQ risks ~$2,000 a contract.
     commission_value = 0.50,
     slippage = 2, pyramiding = 0, calc_on_every_tick = false,
     process_orders_on_close = false)

// KEEP IN SYNC with commission_value above. Pine has no runtime accessor for the configured
// commission, so the label needs its own copy in order to check it against the contract.
commPerOrder = 0.50

// ── preset ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
preset = input.string("30m core", "Preset", options = ["30m core", "60m core", "custom"],
     tooltip = "30m core: range filter 1.0 ATR.  60m core: range filter off. " +
               "Run BOTH on separate charts — that is the tested book. They correlate 0.28.")

grpS = "Structure"
swingK  = input.int(3, "Swing confirmation bars (k)", minval = 1, maxval = 20, group = grpS,
     tooltip = "A pivot needs k bars either side, so it is invisible until k bars later. " +
               "k had a DIFFERENT best value at every timeframe tested — it is the fragile axis. " +
               "Left at the neutral 3 deliberately.")
nBosIn  = input.int(2, "Enter on the Nth BOS", minval = 1, maxval = 5, group = grpS,
     tooltip = "2 is the TESTED value and the lock forces it. But nBos was validated under the " +
               "CHoCH exit, where a second break protected against whipsaw; with a fixed 2R target " +
               "that protection is redundant and the two were never tested jointly. Re-tested, " +
               "nBos=1 with the 2R target wins on nearly everything: research $6,319 vs $2,747, " +
               "LOCKED $11,748 vs $8,932, walk-forward $16,313 vs $11,481 taking 5 of 6 folds, " +
               "Monte Carlo median $18,236 vs $11,558, P(net<0) 0.2% vs 0.7%, paired t +1.55. It " +
               "loses on ONE thing: max drawdown is ~26% deeper (MC median $2,888 vs $2,278, p95 " +
               "$5,166 vs $4,093). Not made the default because t=1.55 is not significant, it came " +
               "from a 9-cell sweep, and it would be the second spec change in a row -- each one " +
               "erodes the claim this rule was not tuned, which is most of why the out-of-sample " +
               "result is believable. Turn OFF the lock to run nBos=1 knowingly. See " +
               "docs/ib/STUDY_CORRELATION_MATRIX.md.")
lockSpec= input.bool(true, "LOCK the tested spec (ignore cached chart settings)", group = grpS,
     tooltip = "TradingView remembers input values PER CHART. If this script was added before a " +
               "revision, your saved values persist and silently override the defaults. With this " +
               "on, nBos, the stop multiple and volatility sizing are forced to the tested values. " +
               "It deliberately does NOT touch the trade-side dropdown or the take-profit: those " +
               "are deliberate choices, and overriding a deliberate choice is the very bug this " +
               "lock exists to catch.")
nBos    = lockSpec ? 2 : nBosIn

sideIn  = input.string("Both", "Trade side", options = ["Both", "Longs only", "Shorts only"],
     group = grpS,
     tooltip = "DEFAULTS TO BOTH, AND SHOULD STAY THERE. Long-only looks better on this data -- " +
               "under the 2R target, longs are 79 trades / $7,990 / PF 1.95 / 48.1% win against " +
               "shorts at 62 trades / $3,689 / PF 1.38 / 38.7%. Do not read that as skill. A " +
               "777,600-cell search over 11 axes found that of every configuration beating the " +
               "tested spec on BOTH the research and locked blocks, 76.5% were long-only and 0.3% " +
               "short-only. What the search was finding is the direction of the 2022-2025 bull " +
               "market. A chronological holdout CANNOT catch that, because both blocks sit inside " +
               "the same regime. Restricting the same search to both-sides cells inverts its " +
               "result completely: locked Sharpe -1.54, losing money out of sample 100% of the " +
               "time. Pick a single side only if you have a reason outside this backtest, and " +
               "size it as a directional bet, not as an edge. This setting is NOT overridden by " +
               "the spec lock -- it takes effect immediately, and the diagnostic label reports it."
)
// NOT under lockSpec. The lock exists to defend against values you did not knowingly set --
// TradingView's per-chart input cache. Picking a side from a dropdown is a deliberate act, and
// silently ignoring it would be the same silent-override bug the lock was built to prevent.
// The setting takes effect; the label calls it what it is.
sideMode = sideIn == "Longs only" ? 1 : sideIn == "Shorts only" ? -1 : 0

grpF = "Filters"
useEma  = input.bool(true, "Require price on the trend side of the EMA", group = grpF)
emaLen  = input.int(200, "EMA length", minval = 10, group = grpF)
mdIn    = input.float(1.0, "Refuse entries within N x ATR of the EMA", minval = 0, step = 0.25, group = grpF)

grpR = "Risk"
atrLen  = input.int(14, "ATR length", minval = 2, group = grpR)
atrMultIn = input.float(2.0, "Stop = N x ATR", minval = 0.25, step = 0.25, group = grpR)
atrMult   = lockSpec ? 2.0 : atrMultIn
tpR     = input.float(2.0, "Take-profit at N x risk  (0 = off)", minval = 0, step = 0.5, group = grpR,
     tooltip = "THE ONE VALIDATED IMPROVEMENT found in this work, and the only change that beat " +
               "the baseline on BOTH the research block and the locked holdout. MNQ 30m: net " +
               "$7,060 -> $11,679, PF 1.53 -> 1.64, win 40.1% -> 44.0%, Sharpe 0.90 -> 1.14 " +
               "(annualised over EVERY session, not just the ~15% that hold a trade -- earlier " +
               "revisions of this file quoted 2.28 -> 2.93, which was inflated ~2.6x). " +
               "Picked in ALL SIX walk-forward folds. It also has a mechanism " +
               "rather than just a number: measured against the driftless barrier bound " +
               "R_down/(R_up+R_down), this signal's excess win rate PEAKS at the 2:1 barrier " +
               "(+10.7 points, vs +6.0 at 1:1 and +8.6 at 3:1). BUT: max drawdown RISES from " +
               "$2,074 to $2,865, and the paired same-session difference against the baseline is " +
               "only t = +1.22 — favourable on every summary statistic, not statistically " +
               "established. Set 0 to reproduce the older reference figures.")
useChoch= input.bool(false, "Exit on the opposite BOS (CHoCH)", group = grpR,
     tooltip = "The original exit. It CONFLICTS with the take-profit above: running both gives " +
               "$8,405 stitched out-of-sample against $11,481 for the target alone, because a " +
               "CHoCH cuts winners before they reach the target. Use ONE of them. " +
               "CHoCH-only (tpR = 0, this ON) is the older tested spec.")

grpX = "ADX / Stochastic  (TESTED — none of these help)"
// Both were tested against this exact book on MNQ 30m and both LOST. They are here so the result
// can be reproduced on your own chart, not because they are recommended. Every default is OFF.
//
//                              research $   LOCKED $      (baseline: $2,747 / $8,932)
//   ADX(14) > 20                    1,661      7,496
//   ADX(14) > 25                    1,016      5,110
//   ADX(14) > 30                     -877      5,014
//   ADX(14) rising 3 bars           3,168      6,262   <- BEST on research, worse on locked
//   Stoch veto K>80 / K<20            902       -711   <- destroys the sample, see below
//   Stoch %K > %D                   3,345      5,370   <- BEST on research, worse on locked
//   a 476-cell sweep of both        3,190      2,138   <- best-on-research; searching cost $6,794
//
// WHY THE STOCHASTIC CANNOT WORK HERE. Measured at the moment each signal fires: LONG signals
// have a median %K of 95.7 with 91.8% above 80; SHORT signals a median of 5.4 with 95.6% below 20.
// A break of structure IS a new extreme of the recent range, and %K measures exactly where the
// close sits in that range, so a bullish BOS prints %K near 100 almost mechanically. Vetoing
// "overbought" longs does not filter this strategy, it DELETES it: 92 research trades become 9,
// and 49 locked trades become 2. The oscillator is measuring the same quantity the entry rule is
// built on. No threshold fixes that.
//
// WHY ADX DOES NOT HELP. As the threshold rises the COUNT falls sharply (92 -> 77 -> 63 -> 43)
// while profit factor and win rate barely move. A filter that changes n without changing the
// profile of what survives is sampling, not selecting -- and randomly sampling a
// positive-expectancy set can only reduce the total. A filter earns its place by making the
// surviving trades BETTER, not fewer.
useAdxMin   = input.bool(false, "Require ADX above a threshold", group = grpX)
adxLen      = input.int(14, "ADX length", minval = 2, group = grpX)
adxMin      = input.float(20.0, "ADX minimum", minval = 0, step = 1, group = grpX)
useAdxRise  = input.bool(false, "Require ADX rising over 3 bars", group = grpX)
useStochVeto= input.bool(false, "Veto overbought longs / oversold shorts", group = grpX,
     tooltip = "This is the one that deletes the strategy. At 80 it cuts 92 trades to 9.")
stochLen    = input.int(14, "Stochastic length", minval = 2, group = grpX)
stochLim    = input.float(80.0, "Veto longs above this %K (mirrored for shorts)", minval = 50,
     maxval = 100, step = 5, group = grpX)
useStochAlign = input.bool(false, "Require %K above %D (mirrored for shorts)", group = grpX)
showOsc     = input.bool(false, "Plot ADX and %K in a pane", group = grpX)

grpV = "Volatility-targeted sizing"
useVTIn = input.bool(false, "Size inversely to recent volatility", group = grpV,
     tooltip = "OFF by default: the reference figures were measured at a FIXED 1 contract, so " +
               "turning this on makes the P&L incomparable to them. It is a separately validated " +
               "overlay — max drawdown 36.3% -> 10.5% while Sharpe ROSE 1.31 -> 1.60 — and it " +
               "changes size only, never direction, so trade count and win rate are unaffected.")
useVT   = lockSpec ? false : useVTIn
vtLook  = input.int(60, "Volatility lookback (bars)", minval = 10, group = grpV)
vtCap   = input.float(2.0, "Maximum size multiplier", minval = 1.0, step = 0.5, group = grpV)
baseQty = input.float(1.0, "Base contracts", minval = 0.1, step = 0.1, group = grpV)

grpT = "Session (exchange time)"
// THE ENTRY GATE. The engine admits an entry only when ALL THREE hold:
//     1. the signal bar is in session
//     2. the NEXT bar is also in session   -- the fill is at the next open, and an entry that
//        fills at 16:00 is filling after the cash close in thin liquidity
//     3. the signal bar is NOT the session's first bar
// Clock arithmetic on the bar's OPEN time in exchange timezone: exact, and no look-ahead, because
// the calendar is known in advance and the prices are not. At 30m this admits 10:00 to 15:00.
// Do NOT go back to an input.session() window — a session string cannot express condition 2,
// which is why the old formulation had to be replaced rather than patched.
sessStart = input.int(570, "Session start (minutes after midnight, exchange time)", group = grpT,
     tooltip = "570 = 09:30. Change only to test a different session.")
sessEnd   = input.int(960, "Session end (minutes after midnight, exchange time)", group = grpT,
     tooltip = "960 = 16:00.")
showSess  = input.bool(true, "Tint the entry window", group = grpT)
flatEOD   = input.bool(false, "Flatten at the session close (intraday-only variant)", group = grpT,
     tooltip = "OFF = the tested spec: a position is held until its stop or a CHoCH, which on 30m " +
               "means a 12.5h median hold and 35% of trades crossing the close. ON = forced flat " +
               "daily. On the full sample flattening looks cheap ($47,997 / PF 1.29 / 44.4% win " +
               "vs $63,165 / PF 1.38 / 39.0%), but split on the research/locked boundary the " +
               "ranking FLIPS: research favours flattening (Sharpe 1.96 vs 1.68), the LOCKED " +
               "third favours holding decisively ($32,642 / Sharpe 1.87 vs $15,182 / 0.95). " +
               "Turn it on for RISK reasons only — no overnight gap exposure, day-trade margin, " +
               "a prop-firm flat-by-close rule — and expect it to cost about HALF the P&L and " +
               "half the Sharpe. It is not an improvement.")

showDiag = input.bool(true, "Show diagnostics (settings actually in force)", group = "Diagnostics",
     tooltip = "On by default. The label on the last bar reports the values IN FORCE, which is " +
               "the only way to catch a cached input silently overriding a default.")

minDist = preset == "30m core" ? 1.0 : preset == "60m core" ? 0.0 : mdIn

// ── indicators ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
emaV = ta.ema(close, emaLen)
atrV = ta.atr(atrLen)

// Confirmed fractal pivots. ta.pivothigh(k,k) returns the pivot k bars back, so reading it on the
// current bar is causal: the value does not exist until the right-hand bars have printed.
ph = ta.pivothigh(high, swingK, swingK)
pl = ta.pivotlow(low,  swingK, swingK)
var float lastPH = na
var float lastPL = na
if not na(ph)
    lastPH := ph
if not na(pl)
    lastPL := pl

// ── structure ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var float brokeHi = na
var float brokeLo = na
var int   bias    = 0
var int   runLen  = 0

// `close` beyond the pivot, not `high`/`low` touching it. The confirmation is load-bearing: on my
// engine, switching to an intrabar touch costs PF 1.49 -> 1.20. See the execution-mode box above.
bosUp = not na(lastPH) and close > lastPH and (na(brokeHi) or lastPH != brokeHi)
bosDn = not na(lastPL) and close < lastPL and (na(brokeLo) or lastPL != brokeLo)
if bosUp
    brokeHi := lastPH
if bosDn
    brokeLo := lastPL
// The counter must be tested against the OLD bias and only then reassigned. Writing `bias := 1`
// first makes the following test trivially true, so the counter never resets on a structure flip.
if bosUp
    if bias == 1
        runLen := runLen + 1
    else
        bias   := 1
        runLen := 1
if bosDn
    if bias == -1
        runLen := runLen + 1
    else
        bias   := -1
        runLen := 1

// ── session gate ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
tfMin    = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period) / 60
barMin   = hour * 60 + minute                          // bar OPEN, exchange timezone
inSess   = barMin >= sessStart and barMin < sessEnd
nextIn   = barMin + tfMin >= sessStart and barMin + tfMin < sessEnd
newSess  = inSess and not inSess[1]
canEnter = inSess and nextIn and not newSess

// ── ADX / Stochastic ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[diPlusV, diMinusV, adxV] = ta.dmi(adxLen, adxLen)
kV = ta.stoch(close, high, low, stochLen)
dV = ta.sma(kV, 3)

// One line each. Pine treats a continuation line indented by a MULTIPLE OF 4 as a new block, so
// `... and\n        (...)` is a syntax error (CE10156) while an odd indent happens to work. Not
// worth relying on that distinction -- these stay on single lines.
adxOk = (not useAdxMin or (not na(adxV) and adxV >= adxMin)) and (not useAdxRise or (not na(adxV) and not na(adxV[3]) and adxV > adxV[3]))
stoLongOk = (not useStochVeto or (not na(kV) and kV <= stochLim)) and (not useStochAlign or (not na(kV) and not na(dV) and kV > dV))
stoShortOk = (not useStochVeto or (not na(kV) and kV >= 100.0 - stochLim)) and (not useStochAlign or (not na(kV) and not na(dV) and kV < dV))

// ── filters ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
farEnough = minDist <= 0 or (not na(emaV) and not na(atrV) and math.abs(close - emaV) >= minDist * atrV)
okLong  = (not useEma or close > emaV) and farEnough
okShort = (not useEma or close < emaV) and farEnough
ready   = not na(atrV) and atrV > 0 and not na(emaV)

longSig = bosUp and runLen >= nBos and okLong and canEnter and ready and sideMode >= 0 and adxOk and stoLongOk
shortSig = bosDn and runLen >= nBos and okShort and canEnter and ready and sideMode <= 0 and adxOk and stoShortOk

// ── volatility-targeted size: bigger when quiet, smaller when wild. Causal. ──────────────────
avgAtr = ta.sma(atrV, vtLook)
vtMult = useVT and not na(avgAtr) and atrV > 0 ? math.min(avgAtr / atrV, vtCap) : 1.0
qty    = math.max(math.round(baseQty * vtMult), 1)

// ── orders ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The stop is anchored to the ACTUAL fill (strategy.position_avg_price), not to the signal bar's
// close. The ATR used is the one that was current when the signal fired.
var float atrAtSignal = na
var float stopLevel   = na

if (longSig or shortSig) and strategy.position_size == 0
    atrAtSignal := atrV

if longSig and strategy.position_size == 0
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long, qty = qty)
if shortSig and strategy.position_size == 0
    strategy.entry("S", strategy.short, qty = qty)

if strategy.position_size > 0 and na(stopLevel)
    stopLevel := strategy.position_avg_price - atrMult * atrAtSignal
if strategy.position_size < 0 and na(stopLevel)
    stopLevel := strategy.position_avg_price + atrMult * atrAtSignal
if strategy.position_size == 0
    stopLevel := na

// Target at tpR x the risk actually taken. Both barriers ride on one exit order, so whichever
// price touches first wins -- and when a single bar spans both, TradingView resolves it the
// conservative way (the stop).
tpLevel = na(stopLevel) or tpR <= 0 ? na :
     strategy.position_size > 0 ? strategy.position_avg_price + tpR * (strategy.position_avg_price - stopLevel) :
     strategy.position_avg_price - tpR * (stopLevel - strategy.position_avg_price)

if strategy.position_size > 0 and not na(stopLevel)
    strategy.exit("Lx", "L", stop = stopLevel, limit = tpLevel)
if strategy.position_size < 0 and not na(stopLevel)
    strategy.exit("Sx", "S", stop = stopLevel, limit = tpLevel)

// Track why positions close, so a win-rate mismatch can be attributed rather than guessed at.
var int nCloses = 0
var int nChoch  = 0
wasIn = strategy.position_size[1] != 0
if wasIn and strategy.position_size == 0
    nCloses += 1

if flatEOD and strategy.position_size != 0 and not inSess
    strategy.close_all(comment = "EOD flat")

if useChoch
    if strategy.position_size > 0 and bosDn
        strategy.close("L", comment = "CHoCH")
        nChoch += 1
    if strategy.position_size < 0 and bosUp
        strategy.close("S", comment = "CHoCH")
        nChoch += 1

if showDiag and barstate.islast
    var label lbl = na
    label.delete(lbl)
    lbl := label.new(bar_index, high,
         "symbol " + syminfo.ticker + "   tf " + timeframe.period +
         "\n$ per point " + str.tostring(syminfo.pointvalue) + "  (NQ 20 / MNQ 2)" +
         "\ncommission $" + str.tostring(commPerOrder) + "/order" +
         (syminfo.pointvalue >= 10 and commPerOrder < 1.5 ?
             "  <<< TOO LOW for an E-mini: set commission_value AND commPerOrder to 2.00" :
          syminfo.pointvalue < 10 and commPerOrder >= 1.5 ?
             "  <<< TOO HIGH for a Micro: set commission_value AND commPerOrder to 0.50" : "  ok") +
         "\n— gates in force —" +
         "\nside " + (sideMode == 0 ? "both" : sideMode == 1 ? "LONGS ONLY (a regime bet)" :
                       "SHORTS ONLY (a regime bet)") +
         "\nnBos " + str.tostring(nBos) + "    stop " + str.tostring(atrMult) + " x ATR" +
         "\ntake-profit " + (tpR > 0 ? str.tostring(tpR) + " R" : "off") +
             "    CHoCH exit " + (useChoch ? "ON" : "off") +
             (tpR > 0 and useChoch ? "   <<< BOTH ON: they conflict, use one" : "") +
         "\nrange filter " + str.tostring(minDist) + " x ATR" +
         "\nentry window " + str.tostring(math.round(sessStart + tfMin)) + "-" +
             str.tostring(math.round(sessEnd - tfMin)) + " (exchange minutes)" +
         "\nvol sizing " + (useVT ? "ON — P&L will not match reference" : "off") +
         "\nADX filter " + (useAdxMin ? ">" + str.tostring(adxMin) : "off") +
             (useAdxRise ? " + rising" : "") +
             "    Stoch " + (useStochVeto ? "veto " + str.tostring(stochLim) : "off") +
             (useStochAlign ? " + K>D" : "") +
             ((useAdxMin or useAdxRise or useStochVeto or useStochAlign) ?
                 "   <<< a tested LOSER is engaged" : "") +
         "\n  ADX now " + (na(adxV) ? "--" : str.tostring(math.round(adxV))) +
             "   %K now " + (na(kV) ? "--" : str.tostring(math.round(kV))) +
         "\n— exits —" +
         "\nposition closes " + str.tostring(nCloses) +
         "\n  of which CHoCH " + str.tostring(nChoch) +
         "\n  of which stops " + str.tostring(nCloses - nChoch) +
         "\n\nIf win rate is near 27%, the stop is running 1xATR, not 2." +
         "\nIf trades are near 290, nBos is running 1, not 2." +
         "\nIf either looks wrong, Script execution must be ON BAR CLOSE ONLY.",
         style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.black, 15), textcolor = color.white)

// ── LIVE ALERTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// alert() inside a bar-close-only script fires ONCE, when the bar closes. Create the alert in
// TradingView with "Once per bar close" -- any other frequency reintroduces the intrabar problem
// the header warns about, because it would fire on an unconfirmed break.
//
// The alert tells you the level to work with; the FILL is the next bar's open, so you place a
// market order when it arrives and take the open. Do not chase, and do not enter mid-bar: an
// entry taken 20 minutes into the bar is not this strategy.
if longSig
    alert("BOS LONG  " + syminfo.ticker + " " + timeframe.period +
          "  |  fill at next open  |  stop " + str.tostring(math.round_to_mintick(close - atrMult * atrV)) +
          "  |  target " + str.tostring(math.round_to_mintick(close + tpR * atrMult * atrV)) +
          "  |  risk " + str.tostring(math.round_to_mintick(atrMult * atrV)) + " pts",
          alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
if shortSig
    alert("BOS SHORT " + syminfo.ticker + " " + timeframe.period +
          "  |  fill at next open  |  stop " + str.tostring(math.round_to_mintick(close + atrMult * atrV)) +
          "  |  target " + str.tostring(math.round_to_mintick(close - tpR * atrMult * atrV)) +
          "  |  risk " + str.tostring(math.round_to_mintick(atrMult * atrV)) + " pts",
          alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

// ── plots ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
plot(emaV, "EMA 200", color.new(color.orange, 0), 2)
bU = plot(minDist > 0 ? emaV + minDist * atrV : na, "no-trade band", color.new(color.gray, 70))
bL = plot(minDist > 0 ? emaV - minDist * atrV : na, "no-trade band", color.new(color.gray, 70))
fill(bU, bL, color.new(color.gray, 92), title = "range — no entries here")
plot(stopLevel, "stop", color.new(color.red, 0), 1, plot.style_linebr)
plot(tpLevel, "target", color.new(color.green, 0), 1, plot.style_linebr)
plotshape(bosUp and inSess, "BOS up",   shape.triangleup,   location.belowbar, color.new(color.teal, 0),   size = size.tiny)
plotshape(bosDn and inSess, "BOS down", shape.triangledown, location.abovebar, color.new(color.maroon, 0), size = size.tiny)
bgcolor(showSess and canEnter ? color.new(color.blue, 94) : na, title = "entry window")

// Optional oscillator pane. Off by default -- these are diagnostics for reproducing the negative
// result above, not inputs to the rule.
plot(showOsc ? adxV : na, "ADX", color.new(color.yellow, 0), 1, display = display.pane)
plot(showOsc ? kV : na, "Stoch %K", color.new(color.aqua, 0), 1, display = display.pane)
plot(showOsc ? dV : na, "Stoch %D", color.new(color.fuchsia, 0), 1, display = display.pane)
hline(showOsc ? 80 : na, "80", color.new(color.gray, 60))
hline(showOsc ? 20 : na, "20", color.new(color.gray, 60))

// =============================================================================================
// WHAT THESE RULES PRODUCED — docs/ib/STUDY_BOS_CHOCH.md, STUDY_LOWER_DD.md, STUDY_MNQ_LIVE.md
//
// NQ, Dec 2022 – Dec 2025, 1 contract, no volatility sizing:
//   30m leg (filter 1.0)   147 trades   +$71,483   PF 1.54   Sharpe 1.00   maxDD 15.6%   t 1.88
//   60m leg (filter 0)      43 trades   +$63,233   PF 2.37   Sharpe 1.08   maxDD 13.1%
//   LOCKED final third (268 sessions, never used to choose anything):
//                            30m +$47,072 Sharpe 1.39   |   60m +$28,065 Sharpe 1.07
//
// MNQ at $0.50/order, same window — and note the LOCKED block beats the research block, which is
// rare and is the strongest single thing that can be said for this rule:
//   full sample   147 trades  +$7,060  PF 1.53  40.1% win  maxDD $2,074  Sharpe 0.90
//   research 65%   91 trades  +$2,387  PF 1.34               maxDD $1,245  Sharpe 0.64
//   LOCKED   35%   56 trades  +$4,674  PF 1.75               maxDD $2,074  Sharpe 1.26
//   with the 2R target: 141 trades +$11,679 PF 1.64 44.0% win maxDD $2,865  Sharpe 1.14
//
// SHARPE CORRECTION. Earlier revisions of this file quoted 2.28 / 3.07 / 2.93. Those were
// annualised over TRADING DAYS ONLY -- the 137-147 sessions that held a trade -- while the rule is
// flat on ~85% of days. Annualising a sparse series over sqrt(252) as if every day looked like a
// trading day inflates Sharpe by about 2.6x. The figures above now include every session in the
// block, with zero on days that did not trade, which is the standard construction. Dollars,
// profit factor, win rate and drawdown were never affected.
//
// Monte Carlo, 5,000 stationary-block-bootstrap paths on daily P&L:
//   net $   p5 1,232   median 6,969   p95 13,214        P(net < 0) = 2.3%
//   maxDD   p5 1,337   median 2,180   p95  3,922        P(DD > 2x realised) = 3.4%
//
// WHAT FAILS, so you do not rediscover it:
//   • 1m and 2m are SIGNIFICANTLY NEGATIVE (t = −2.45, −2.68). This does not scalp.
//   • At 5m, coin-flip entries with the same risk management beat it 65% of the time.
//   • Adding a 15m leg RAISED book drawdown to 37% for a Calmar of 0.26. Two legs, not three.
//   • An equity-curve brake (cut size after an 8% drawdown) made everything worse.
//   • SIX pre-specified variants — confirmation buffers, breakeven stops, time-of-day windows —
//     were tested with a locked holdout. NONE beat the baseline on both blocks. The rank
//     correlation between research and locked Sharpe was −0.429: the variant that looked WORST
//     in research was the BEST out of sample. Walk-forward re-selection cost −$3,857 against
//     never changing anything. Do not tune this. Tuning it is the documented way to lose money.
//   • PBO is 0.571 and t = 1.88 against a ~2.7 multiple-testing hurdle. The strategy is
//     profitable and out-of-sample positive; it is NOT a statistically established edge.
//   • There is NO ES DATA behind any of this. One instrument, one bull market, three years.
//
// SIZE: a 2×ATR stop on 30m NQ risks ~$2,000 per contract; on MNQ ~$200. If you switch to
// full-size NQ or ES, change BOTH `commission_value` (line ~55) and `commPerOrder` to 2.00 —
// the label will tell you if you forget.
//
// Research tooling for education and analysis. Not financial advice.
// =============================================================================================
